// Juxtaposing a parameter does not make it a String.
//
// The analyzer recorded `CompatibleWith(String)` for any parameter that
// appeared in a juxtaposition, resolved it to String, and then rejected every
// other type at the call site:
//
//     g(b) { <~ "[" b "]" }
//     >> g(42) ¶      // error: argument 1 has type Int, but 'g' expects String
//
// one line away from `>> "[" n "]" ¶` with the same 42, which printed `[42]`.
// Both Rust engines refused a correct program; the browser engine ran it. A
// divergence that REJECTS rather than mis-runs, so no golden could see it —
// only a program that failed to compile in two engines out of three.
//
// Any function that builds a message out of a number was affected, which is
// most functions that build a message. ZyBank hit it and the report blamed
// juxtaposition in call arguments (GAP-ZYB-007); that part was never true —
// `f("a" resto)` has always worked.
etiquetar(v) { <~ "[" v "]" }

>> etiquetar("texto") ¶
>> etiquetar(42) ¶
>> etiquetar(3.5) ¶
>> etiquetar(#1) ¶
>> etiquetar('z') ¶
>> etiquetar([1, 2]) ¶
>> etiquetar((x: 1)) ¶

// Two parameters, one of each, and the juxtaposition in the middle.
unir(a, b) { <~ a " y " b }
>> unir("uno", 2) ¶
>> unir(1, "dos") ¶

// The arithmetic constraint still holds: this one really is numeric.
doblar(n) { <~ n * 2 }
>> doblar(21) ¶
